# Local development variables and secrets for `wrangler dev`.
#
# Names only; values are intentionally omitted. Never put real secrets in this
# file or in .dev.vars (which is committed and sets DEV_MODE=true). Put them in
# .dev.vars.local, which is gitignored.
#
# Loading: plain `wrangler dev` (npm run dev) reads ONLY .dev.vars. It does not
# read .dev.vars.local (verified with wrangler 4.76.0). To load both, pass them
# explicitly; a later --env-file overrides an earlier one (verified):
#   npx wrangler dev --env-file .dev.vars --env-file .dev.vars.local
#
# Non-secret vars come from [vars] in wrangler.toml and only need an entry here
# to override them locally: CF_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN, CF_ACCESS_AUDIENCE,
# CF_ACCESS_INVITE_AUDIENCE, BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL, EXTERNAL_EVAL_KEY_ID, and
# the Stage 2 vars below (committed as empty strings, so Stage 2 is off).
#
# Bindings (no entry needed): DB (local D1), OAUTH_KV (local KV),
# EVAL_RATE_LIMITER, MCP_RATE_LIMITER, OAUTH_RATE_LIMITER are all simulated
# locally by wrangler dev from wrangler.toml.

# --- Vars: existing app ---

# "true" bypasses Cloudflare Access verification; requests identify the user
# with the X-Dev-User-Email header. Local only, never set in production.
# Used by the REST API and the Stage 1 MCP endpoint (/api/mcp).
# DEV_MODE=

# --- Secrets: existing app (production: npx wrangler secret put <NAME>) ---

# RSA private key (PKCS#8 PEM) that signs External Evaluation response JWTs.
# Only needed locally when exercising /api/eval/*.
# EXTERNAL_EVAL_PRIVATE_KEY=

# --- Vars: Stage 2, OAuth authorization server on the MCP hostname ---
#
# Leave MCP_HOST empty (the default) and Stage 2 is disabled: every request is
# served by the existing app. To try Stage 2 locally, use a DEV Access for SaaS
# OIDC application (never the production one):
#   1. Zero Trust -> Access -> Applications -> Add -> SaaS -> OIDC. Add the
#      redirect URL http://localhost:8787/callback. Give it a policy that lets
#      your account in.
#   2. Copy its endpoints and credentials into .dev.vars.local (below).
#   3. Set MCP_HOST=localhost and MCP_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:8787/mcp.
#   4. Run:
#        npx wrangler dev --env-file .dev.vars --env-file .dev.vars.local \
#          --local-upstream localhost:8787
#      Why --local-upstream: wrangler dev rewrites every request URL to one
#      host, by default the host of the first route in wrangler.toml (the
#      production app host), whatever host you browse to (verified). With
#      --local-upstream localhost:8787 the Worker sees http://localhost:8787/...,
#      which matches MCP_HOST and MCP_PUBLIC_URL. That process then serves
#      only Stage 2; run a plain `npm run dev` (other port) for the web app.
#   5. Connect the MCP Inspector or
#      `claude mcp add --transport http trips-dev http://localhost:8787/mcp`.

# Bare hostname the dispatcher matches (no scheme, port or path). Empty = off.
# Locally: localhost (with --local-upstream, see above). Production: injected
# from the MCP_HOST GitHub secret.
# MCP_HOST=

# Public MCP endpoint, the OAuth protected-resource identifier. Must equal the
# URL entered in the client exactly. Locally: http://localhost:8787/mcp.
# MCP_PUBLIC_URL=

# Access for SaaS OIDC endpoints, copied from the app's page in the dashboard
# (shape: https://<team>.cloudflareaccess.com/cdn-cgi/access/sso/oidc/<client-id>/...).
# ACCESS_AUTHORIZATION_URL=
# ACCESS_TOKEN_URL=
# ACCESS_JWKS_URL=

# Expected `iss` of the Access for SaaS ID token (the app's Issuer value).
# ACCESS_ISSUER=

# --- Secrets: Stage 2 (production: npx wrangler secret put <NAME>) ---

# Client ID and client secret of the Access for SaaS OIDC application. The
# Worker is a confidential client to Access (and also sends PKCE).
# ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=
# ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=
